          Health Assessment of Higher Education
               System in Three-dimensions




                                                          型
                                          Summary




                                                        模
      Education is an essential issue for any countries in any time, in which the higher
  education determines the height of the national education development and matters a




                                                       学
  lot in almost all the other areas and industries. Therefore the health and sustainability
  of the national higher education system is required to be evaluated comprehensively.
     Firstly, 20 sets of data for 22 countries are collected and integrated to 12 indices. We




                                                     数
  divide these 12 indices into three dimensions which are School Development (SD), Stu-
  dent Treatment (ST), and Social Inﬂuence (SI). We combine the Group Decision Making
                                                  ：
  Method (GDM) with the Entropy Weight Method (EWM) to calculate the weight of
  these indices in the three dimensions. Then we build the Three-dimensional Vector




                                                 els
  Assessment Model (3DVA). The three dimensions values as components of an assess-
                           号
  ment vector are placed in a three-dimensional coordinate system. The modulus of the



                                               od
  vector represents the comprehensive health level of the higher education system while
  the deviation angle to the standard vector (1, 1, 1) indicates the balanced sustainable
                          众


  condition. 8 countries are assessed by the model. The UK ranks ﬁrst in sustainability
                                             Hm
  with the smallest deviation angle of 1.15◦ and the US ranks ﬁrst in health level with
  the longest modulus of 1.278. India, with a modulus of 0.735 and an angle of 23.13◦ ,
                  公



  has a lot of room for development. Therefore we choose India for further discussion.
      Secondly, we establish the Curved-surface Target Model (CST) to conﬁgure the
                                          AT
          信




  achievable vision for India. We combine 3 comprehensive authoritative indicators as
  non-educational factors and attain two indicators, Em and Eθ . Based on Indian current
  vector, Em lengthens the modulus and Eθ reduces the deviation angle within a certain
  微

                                     M



  range. Combining the modulus and angle, a curved-surface can be formed, according
  to which, we propose policies for India that are divided into three terms. During the
  short and medium terms, policies will help India to achieve balanced development in
                     ：




  the three dimensions (reducing the angle to 11.5◦ ). In the long term, the comprehensive
  health level of India will be enhanced (increasing the modulus to 1.22).
                    号




     Thirdly, we introduce the N-Verhulst Model (NV) to predict the development
  without our policy intervention in 20 years. Compared with intervened one, we dis-
  cover that without policies application, Student Treatment will get worse, the angle
           信




  will be 29.96◦ and the modulus will be 0.640. After intervention, the situation will be
  improved. The ﬁnal modulus will change to 0.978 and the angle will be 4.86◦ , which
   微




  proves the validity of our policies.
      In the end, we analyze the inﬂuence of changing the higher education system, re-
  alizing that changes are difﬁcult.
  Keywords: Higher Education System; Health and Sustainability; Policy; GDM; EWM;
  Three-dimensional Vector; N-Verhulst


